          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 13 — Our Home: Earth, a Unique Life Sustaining Planet"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.896,
            0.038,
            0.932,
            0.053
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "219"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.083,
            0.546,
            0.251
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are various types of landforms, rocks, soils, etc., on Earth. This variety along with the processes that shape and alter them is called geodiversity (Fig. 13.9). It helps create unique habitats where different types of life can thrive. The non-living parts of nature, like soil, rocks, and water, aren’t just a background—they help shape the story of life itself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.552,
            0.065,
            0.94,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.552,
                0.065,
                0.94,
                0.238
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.649,
                0.24,
                0.829,
                0.257
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 13.9: Geodiversity"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.261,
            0.611,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.4.3 Plants, animals, and microorganisms"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.284,
            0.744,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the chapter on microbes to the chapter on ecology, we have seen that the Earth is full of life — from trees, shrubs, herbs, to animals, insects, and tiny organisms invisible to the naked eye. All living beings, along with the places where they live, make up the biosphere. This includes land, water, and air, where life interacts with its surroundings to survive and grow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.397,
            0.744,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As you learnt in Chapter 12, living beings depend on one another and their environment. Plants make food through photosynthesis, animals eat plants or other animals, and decomposers break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil. Nature works together as a system to support life."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.501,
            0.495,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.4.4 The importance of balance"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.524,
            0.744,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Have you ever wondered how so many things on Earth stay in balance? Earth is like a giant teamwork project between nature, weather, and life itself. It is a vast, living system where land, air, water, and living things support and affect one another. Even a small change in one part—like cutting down a forest—can impact rainfall, soil, air quality, and the animals that live there. Life on Earth survives not because of just one thing, but because everything works together in balance. It is this balance that keeps our planet habitable. That’s why preserving and protecting clean air, water, soil, and all forms of life isn’t just important but essential for keeping Earth healthy for the future."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.743,
            0.676,
            0.769
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.5 What Keeps Life from Disappearing?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.783,
            0.744,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If plants and animals didn’t reproduce, life would eventually disappear from Earth. Reproduction ensures that each type of organism continues to exist, maintaining the continuity of life."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.841,
            0.745,
            0.917
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We usually expect that animals will produce young ones that resemble them—cows have calves, and cats have kittens. This happens because parents pass on instructions to their offspring about how to develop from a single cell. These instructions,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 14,
